        "original_id": "677079fe-2406-5d09-8f0f-73a1b7d96726",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Herbert Matthews, Fulgencio Batista, Fidel Castro, Ruby Hart Phillips\n- Organizations: The New York Times, 26th of July Movement\n- Places: Sierra Maestra, Cuba\n- Events: Cuban Revolution\n\nFacts:\n- Herbert Matthews was born on January 10, 1900, and later worked as a reporter and editorialist for The New York Times.\n- Fulgencio Batista was born on January 16, 1901, as Rubén Zaldívar.\n- Fulgencio Batista served as the elected President of Cuba from 1940 to 1944.\n- Fulgencio Batista was the U.S.-backed dictator of Cuba from 1952 to 1959.\n- During the 26th of July Movement's landing, Fulgencio Batista publicly claimed that Fidel Castro had been killed.\n- Herbert Matthews later revealed that Fidel Castro was alive and living in the Sierra Maestra mountains.\n- Ruby Hart Phillips's coverage was relatively favorable toward Fulgencio Batista.\n- Ruby Hart Phillips's coverage often conflicted with that of Herbert Matthews, a noted Times foreign correspondent who favored Fidel Castro.\n- Fulgencio Batista was overthrown during the Cuban Revolution.\n- Fulgencio Batista died on August 6, 1973.\n- Herbert Matthews died on July 30, 1977.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "94b83e88-a912-5688-bcad-216291ea3de7",
        "text": "Page: Herbert Matthews. Sentence: Herbert Lionel Matthews (January 10, 1900 – July 30, 1977) was a reporter and editorialist for \"The New York Times\" who grew to notoriety after revealing that Fidel Castro was still alive and living in the Sierra Maestra mountains, though Fulgencio Batista had claimed publicly that he was killed during the 26th of July Movement's landing.\nPage: Fulgencio Batista. Sentence: Fulgencio Batista y Zaldívar (] ; born Rubén Zaldívar; January 16, 1901 – August 6, 1973) was the elected President of Cuba from 1940 to 1944, and U.S.-backed dictator from 1952 to 1959, before being overthrown during the Cuban Revolution.\nPage: Ruby Hart Phillips. Sentence: Her coverage, relatively favorable toward Batista, was often at odds with that of Herbert Matthews, the noted \"Times\" foreign correspondent who favored Castro.",
